  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Derivatives of exponential</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the derivative of the following function.</p>
<p>\(f(x)= \displaystyle {@f_ction@}\)</p>
<p>\(f^{\prime}(x)=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]] </p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><h3>Worked solution</h3></p>
<p>\(f(x)= \displaystyle {@f_ction@}\)</p>
<p>The function \(f\) is in the form \(\displaystyle v{@%e^u@} \), whose derivative is given by the formula</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle v'{@%e^u@}+v{({@%e^u@}})' \). But \( \displaystyle {({@%e^u@}})'= \displaystyle u' {@%e^u@}\)</p>
<p> Here, \(v={@v_x@}\) and \(u={@u_x@}\). The derivatives of \(u\) and \(v\) with respect to \(x\) are therefore;</p>
<p> \( v'= \displaystyle {@diff(v_x,x)@}\) and  \( u'= \displaystyle {@diff(u_x,x)@}\). Since \(f'(x)= \displaystyle v' \cdot {@%e^u@}+v \cdot u' \cdot {@%e^u@}\), we have </p>
<p>\(f'(x)= \displaystyle {@ta@}\) </p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
